/-! # Cohomology of a colimit of sites with finite coverings: morphism of sites and descent
(Stacks 0A35, 0EXJ)

The second of the four files on the cohomology of a colimit of sites with finite coverings
(see `CohomologyOfColimitOfFiniteSites.AbelianSystemsAndSections`). It shows that the functors
`u_i` into the colimit site are morphisms of sites, with the sections of the pullbacks computed by
a filtered colimit (Stacks 0A35), and that a sheaf on the colimit site is the colimit of the
pullbacks of its pushforwards (Stacks 0EXJ). Each half carries its own description below.

## Main results

* `InverseSystemOfFiniteSites.exists_isMorphismOfSites_sections_pullback_of_colimit`:
  **Stacks 0A35** for an inverse system of sites with finite coverings.
* `InverseSystemOfFiniteSites.isColimit_pullback_pushforward_of_colimit`
  and the `pullbackPushforwardDesc` family: **Stacks 0EXJ** for such a system.
-/

/-! # The colimit of an inverse system of sites, as a morphism of sites

Let `D` be an inverse system of sites with finite coverings (Stacks 0A34): a cofiltered index
category `D.I`, for each `i` a site `(D.C i, D.J i)` all of whose coverings have a finite index
set, and for each `a : i ⟶ j` a continuous functor `D.u a : D.C j ⥤ D.C i`, compatibly with
composition. Let the functors `u i : D.C i ⥤ C` exhibit `C` as the colimit of the underlying
categories and let `K` be the collection of coverings of `C` produced by Stacks 09YL, namely the
images under the `u i` of the finite coverings of the `D.C i`.

Each `u i` is then continuous for `(D.J i)` and the topology generated by `K`, and the induced
functor `f_i^{-1} = u_{i,s}` on sheaves of sets is exact: that is, `u i` defines a morphism of
sites `f_i : C → C_i` in the sense of Stacks 00X1. Moreover the sections of `f_i^{-1} F` over an
object coming from level `i` are computed by the filtered colimit over the arrows into `i`
$$
f_i^{-1}\mathcal F(u_i(U)) = \operatorname*{colim}_{a : j → i} f_a^{-1}\mathcal F(u_a(U)),
$$
which is equation (09YM) of the source and the identity from which exactness is deduced there.

## Main results

* `InverseSystemOfFiniteSites.exists_isMorphismOfSites_sections_pullback_of_colimit`: for the
  colimit site of Stacks 09YL, each `u i` defines a morphism of sites `f_i : C → C_i`, and the
  sections of `f_i^{-1} F` over `u_i U` are the filtered colimit of the sections of `f_a^{-1} F`
  over `u_a U` along the canonical maps (Stacks 0A35, including equation 09YM).

## Implementation notes

* Morphism of sites is `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsMorphismOfSites`, this repository's Stacks 00X1:
  continuity of `u i` together with exactness of `f_i^{-1} = u_i.sheafPullback (Type w)`. Since
  the exactness half only makes sense once `u i` is known to be continuous, and equation (09YM)
  mentions `f_i^{-1}` as well, the whole conclusion sits under `∃ hf : ∀ i, …`, the continuity
  instance being extracted from `hf` by `haveI`. Both sites are small and the sheaves are sheaves
  of sets, which is the shape of the Stacks 00X1 API of this repository (and of Stacks 00VH, whose
  sites are categories with a set of objects); it is also what makes `f_i^{-1}` available, as
  `HasSheafify` for `Type w`-valued presheaves on a small site is what produces the left adjoint.
* The colimit of equation (09YM) is a colimit over the category of arrows `a : j ⟶ i` of `D.I`,
  which is cofiltered because `D.I` is, so the colimit indexing category is filtered. A filtered
  colimit of sets is described elementwise, and that is how (09YM) is stated here: the canonical
  maps `θ a` are jointly surjective, and two sections at the same level with the same image become
  equal at some further level. Stating it this way needs only the *legs* `θ a` of the colimit cone,
  never the transition maps of the diagram, and the transition maps are the one part of the data
  that cannot be written down without the comparison isomorphism
  `f_{b ∘ a}^{-1} ≅ f_b^{-1} ∘ f_a^{-1}`; "becoming equal at a further level" is therefore phrased
  with the unit of the `D.u b`-adjunction applied to `f_a^{-1} F`, which is that transition map
  composed with an isomorphism, hence has the same kernel.
* The legs are pinned, not quantified over: `θ` is an arbitrary family subject to `hθ`, which
  identifies `θ a` with the canonical map — the adjoint of the unit of the `u i`-adjunction under
  the `D.u a`-adjunction, read on sections over `U` through `f_{i,*}f_i^{-1}F(U) =
  f_i^{-1}F(u_i U)` and `f_{a,*}f_a^{-1}F(U) = f_a^{-1}F(u_a U)`. Quantifying over `θ` only avoids
  writing that expression three times; `∀ θ, θ = e → P θ` and `P e` are the same statement.
  The identification of the composite `f_{a,*}f_{j,*}` with `f_{i,*}` used there is
  `Functor.sheafPushforwardContinuousComp'` at `eqToIso (hu a)`; on the pushforward side this
  comparison is the identity, which is why the canonical map is written on that side.
* That `C` is the colimit of the `D.C i` is spelled out as `hobj`, `hhom` and `hhom_inj`, and the
  coverings of `C` as `hK`, exactly as in
  `InverseSystemOfFiniteSites.exists_precoverage_of_colimit` (Stacks 09YL), whose
  conclusion supplies them; the topology of `C` is the one they generate, `K.toGrothendieck`.

On faithfulness:
* No hypothesis is dead: `hu`, `hobj`, `hhom`, `hhom_inj` say that `u` exhibits `C` as the colimit
  of the system and `hK` says which families are coverings of `C`; drop any one and neither
  continuity of `u i` nor (09YM) holds — `hu` moreover enters the conclusion itself, through the
  identification of `u_j(u_a U)` with `u_i U` that defines the canonical maps.
* The data is the source's: the sites, the cofiltered index category and the finiteness of the
  coverings are those of `D`, whose transition functors are its continuous `D.u a`; the sheaves are
  sheaves of sets on `(D.C i, D.J i)` and on `C` with the topology generated by the coverings of
  Stacks 09YL, and `f_i^{-1}`, `f_a^{-1}` are the pullbacks along `u i`, `D.u a`.
* The canonical maps are pinned by `hθ` and by the use of the adjunction unit in the separatedness
  clause; the statement does not merely assert that some family of maps has these properties.
* The instantiation is not degenerate: take `D.I` the one-object category, `D.C` a single small
  category with the trivial topology, `D.u` the identity functor, `C` that category and `u` the
  identity functor; then `hu`–`hK` hold, `u i` is the identity, which is a morphism of sites, and
  (09YM) is the statement that a one-term filtered colimit is what it is.

## References

* [Stacks 0A35](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A35)
-/

